      "bounds": "Two trusted queue scopes; three possible pre-existing row IDs plus one additional fresh ID; each row weight1\u20132 abstract payload units. MaxRows2/MaxBytes3. Initial unique-ID row sets include legacy states with3rows and up to6units, beyond either cap. Initial enumeration uses the exact729 unique-ID assignments, not a powerset filter. No time transition: expiry is a pre-existing Boolean classification.",
      "properties": [
        "TypeOK",
        "FailedSendPreservesRows",
        "ForeignRowsPreserved",
        "LiveRowsPreserved",
        "BudgetAccountsPhysical",
        "PhysicalRowCap",
        "PayloadByteCap",
        "ReadRemovesOwnExpired",
        "DeleteScopeExact",
        "MaintenanceNotBudgetBlocked"
      ],
      "limits": "Finite physical-payload budget abstraction, not Go/SQLite refinement, SQL transaction/crash proof, Unicode/UTF8/string-byte counting, JSON attribute serialization, actual64MiB/10000row arithmetic, file size/SQLite overhead, clock/retention, receipt/IAM authorization or random-ID collision resistance. Row weights abstract persisted body+serialized-attribute bytes; all foreign rows, including expired ones, consume budget. Scopes/parent queues are assumed already valid and trusted; SQSLifecycle/native tests separately handle authority, parent lifetime and receipts. Maintenance enabledness assumes these preconditions, not unauthorized access or liveness/fairness. Charged counters describe successful admission accounting, not a claim that an actual storage-error path computed counters. FailedSendPreservesRows still applies to every rejected/storage-error send. Legacy overcap rows are retained: only new accepted sends must fit both caps, own expired cleanup is explicit/scoped and rollback preserves every row on send failure.",
      "source_scope": [
        "internal/aws/sqs_store.go"
      ],
